Do not reference MSG_NOSIGNAL on macOS; it does not exist before macOS 11.

MSG_NOSIGNAL is a Linux extension that Darwin only picked up in macOS 11
(Darwin 20). On older SDKs `C.MSG_NOSIGNAL` reaches the C compiler as a plain
undeclared identifier, and the generated `_vinit` fails with

    error: 'MSG_NOSIGNAL' undeclared (first use in this function)
        _const_net__msg_nosignal = MSG_NOSIGNAL;

macOS suppresses SIGPIPE with the SO_NOSIGPIPE socket option instead, and
TcpSocket.set_default_options() already sets it under `$if macos` for every
socket it creates or adopts, so passing 0 as the send/recv flag loses nothing
there. 0 is also what vlib/net/net_windows.c.v uses, and what
vlib/fasthttp/fasthttp_bsd.c.v already falls back to on the BSDs that have
SO_NOSIGPIPE -- that file gates the flag the same way, with `$if openbsd`.

A C-level `#ifndef MSG_NOSIGNAL` guard in the .c.v file would be the narrower
fix, but V 0.5.2 only accepts `#define`, `#flag`, `#include`, `#insert` and
`#pkgconfig` directives in V source (vlib/v/checker/checker.v:4530), and its
cgen drops every other kind silently (vlib/v/gen/c/cgen.v:11026), so the
comptime-if is the portable way to express this here.

--- vlib/net/net_nix.c.v.original
+++ vlib/net/net_nix.c.v
@@ -20,7 +20,7 @@
 fn init() {
 }
 
-pub const msg_nosignal = C.MSG_NOSIGNAL
+pub const msg_nosignal = $if macos { 0 } $else { C.MSG_NOSIGNAL }
 pub const msg_dontwait = C.MSG_DONTWAIT
 
 pub const error_ewouldblock = int(C.EWOULDBLOCK)
